  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed explanation of why men tend to have larger Adam's apples:\n\n### What Is an Adam's Apple?\n\nThe Adam's apple is a prominence of the thyroid cartilage that partially encloses the larynx \u2014 i.e., the voice box. Also known as the laryngeal prominence, the Adam's apple sits right on top of the thyroid gland, so the area is fittingly called the thyroid cartilage.\n\n### Do Women Have One Too?\n\nYes, women do have Adam's apples, but it's usually much less prominent than in men. Both men and women have the same structures in their throats, including the thyroid cartilage, but in women, it tends to be smaller and less noticeable.\n\n### Why Is It Bigger in Men?\n\nThe key reason comes down to **puberty and testosterone**:\n\n- The Adam's apple is about the same size in people assigned male at birth (AMAB) and those assigned female at birth (AFAB) until puberty. Once puberty starts, hormones (especially testosterone) in people AMAB may cause their vocal cords to grow bigger. The thyroid cartilage may therefore stick out a bit more to protect these larger vocal cords.\n\n- Testosterone causes the male larynx to grow significantly during puberty, pushing the thyroid cartilage outward and creating that visible bump on the throat.\n\n### Why Does a Bigger Larynx = Deeper Voice?\n\nPeople with a larger Adam's apple tend to have a deeper voice than people with a smaller one, because some people develop more cartilage around the vocal cords, or they have a larger voice box.\n\n### Shape Differences Also Play a Role\n\nThere is also a difference in the shape of the laryngeal prominence in males and females. In males, the sides of the laryngeal prominence protrude at an almost 90\u00b0 angle, while in females, it is at a more obtuse 120\u00b0 angle, making it appear less visible.\n\n### An Evolutionary Angle\n\nResearchers have proposed that a larger larynx originally allowed individuals to produce vocalizations that mimic a larger body size. In humans, a deeper, more resonant voice may have helped adult males sound more imposing relative to females and younger males, giving them an edge in social competition.\n\n### Why Do Some Women Have a Noticeable One?\n\nSome women with a larger larynx do have a noticeable Adam's apple, but this indicates the size of the voice box only. In some women, a larger larynx may be caused by an increased amount of testosterone, which is also responsible for other body changes, such as body hair. Body composition also plays a major role: women with less subcutaneous fat around the neck will show more of the underlying cartilage structure.\n\nIn summary, men have larger Adam's apples primarily because testosterone during puberty causes their larynx and surrounding cartilage to grow much more than in women \u2014 a change that also results in a deeper voice.",
